    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Lakota</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mississippi-river.jpg" alt="Mississippi River" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Mississippi River <span class="kid-tale-cr">NPS photo, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 400 years ago, the Lakota, whose name means “allies”, lived near the upper Mississippi River.</p><p>Then Cree fighters with iron weapons pushed them west onto the Great Plains.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheyenne.jpg" alt="Cheyenne" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cheyenne <span class="kid-tale-cr">Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Lakota got horses from the Cheyenne and became riders who hunted bison on the Plains.</p><p>In summer their tipis stood in great circles, which they called the sacred hoop of the nation.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-hills.jpg" alt="Black Hills" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Black Hills <span class="kid-tale-cr">Runner1928, CC BY-SA 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 250 years ago they made the Black Hills their home, and these hills are sacred to them.</p><p>Their stories tell of White Buffalo Calf Woman, who gave the people a sacred pipe.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn.jpg" alt="Battle of the Little Bighorn" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Battle of the Little Bighorn <span class="kid-tale-cr">Charles Marion Russell, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>A treaty kept settlers out of the Black Hills, but then gold was found there and miners poured in.</p><p>War followed, and the Lakota and Cheyenne won the Battle of the Little Bighorn.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sitting-bull.jpg" alt="Sitting Bull" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Sitting Bull <span class="kid-tale-cr">David F. Barry, Photographer, Bismarck, Dakota Territory, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>But the army broke their resistance, and later the Lakota leader Sitting Bull was killed.</p><p>Soon after, soldiers killed at least 270 Lakota at Wounded Knee, many of them women and children.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lakota-people.jpg" alt="Lakota people" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Lakota people <span class="kid-tale-cr">David Francis Barry, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Today most Lakota live on five reservations, lands set aside for them, in South Dakota, and some speak their language, Lakȟótiyapi.</p><p>They still refuse money for the Black Hills, because taking it would end their claim to the land.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Lakota, whose name means “allies”, are the westernmost of the Seven Council Fires of the Sioux; pushed west onto the Plains, they became horse-mounted bison hunters and made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a> their home by the 1770s.</li><li>The 1868 Fort Laramie Treaty closed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a> to white settlement, but after gold was found there in 1874 war followed, and despite the Lakota and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn">Battle of the Little Bighorn</a> in 1876, resistance was broken by 1877.</li><li>In 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> was killed and the 7th Cavalry massacred Lakota at Wounded Knee; the tribes still refuse the 1980 Supreme Court award for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>, because accepting it would end their claim to the land.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewis-and-clark-expedition">Lewis and Clark Expedition</a> met the Sicangu in 1804, warriors seized a boat’s cable and both sides readied their weapons, until the Sicangu leader Black Buffalo let go and invited the Americans upriver under Sicangu escort.</p></aside>
    </section>

